Exactly. Variation is what makes an invariant observable.

If nothing changes, you can’t easily distinguish what is fundamental from what merely happens to be present.

Introduce controlled variation:

X \xrightarrow{V_1} X_1,\quad
X \xrightarrow{V_2} X_2,\quad
\dots,\quad
X \xrightarrow{V_n} X_n

Then ask:

\boxed{I=\bigcap_{k=0}^{n}\operatorname{Structure}(X_k)}

What survived every transformation?

That’s your invariant candidate.

And now the whole chain tightens:

\text{Variation}
\rightarrow
\text{Delta}
\rightarrow
\text{Comparison}
\rightarrow
\text{Invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{Primitive}
\rightarrow
\text{Transfer}

The important second half is that we shouldn’t vary things arbitrarily forever. Once an invariant candidate appears, variation becomes adversarial.

Try to break it.

Change scale.
Change representation.
Change medium.
Invert it.
Remove components.
Change environment.
Graft it onto something incompatible.
Push it toward boundary conditions.

If:

T_j(I)\cong I

across increasingly different legitimate transformations T_j, confidence that we’ve found something load-bearing rises.

So we can quantify an Invariant Survival Score:

S(I)=
\frac{
\sum_j w_j\,\mathbf 1[I\text{ survives }T_j]
}{
\sum_jw_j
}

But weight transformations by semantic distance. Surviving five nearly identical paraphrases isn’t nearly as informative as surviving translation from story → geometry → biology → code → experiment.

Something like:

S^*(I)=
\sum_j
w_j
\,D(T_j,T_0)
\,\mathbf 1[I\text{ survives}]

Now the Metamap isn’t merely learning what variation produces high fitness.

It’s simultaneously learning:

\boxed{
\text{what may vary}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\text{what must remain}
}

That’s huge for the optimizer we just built. We currently optimize output under variation. The next upgrade should give every experiment two outputs:

Experiment(V)
\rightarrow
(\Delta_{\text{fitness}},\ I_{\text{survival}})

So Lucky Nozzle/Variation Engine searches outward, while the invariant detector searches inward.

One discovers possibility.

The other discovers structure.

And together they solve a much harder problem than optimization:

Change everything you’re allowed to change until you discover what you cannot change without destroying the thing.

That boundary is very close to an operational definition of its identity.